@article{ArrowDebreuExchange2024,
 abstract = {In theoretical economics, an Arrow--Debreu exchange market is a special case of the Arrow--Debreu model in which there is no production - there is only an exchange of already-existing goods.  An Arrow--Debreu exchange market has the following ingredients: A set of                         m                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle m\}     divisible products. A set of                         n                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle n\}     agents. Each agent                         i         =         1         ,         {\dots}         ,         n                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i=1,{\textbackslash}dots ,n\}    , has an endowment                                    e                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle e\_\{i\}\}    , which is a set of products. Each product                         j                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle j\}     has a price                                    p                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p\_\{j\}\}    ; the prices are determined by methods described below. The price of a bundle of products is the sum of the prices of the products in the bundle. A bundle is represented by a vector                         x         =                    x                        1                             ,         {\dots}         ,                    x                        m                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x=x\_\{1\},{\textbackslash}dots ,x\_\{m\}\}    , where                                    x                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\_\{j\}\}     is the quantity of product                         j                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle j\}    . So the price of a bundle                         x                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\}     is                         p         {$\cdot$}         x         =                    {$\sum$}                        j             =             1                                   m                                        p                        j                             {$\cdot$}                    x                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p{\textbackslash}cdot x={\textbackslash}sum \_\{j=1\}{\textasciicircum}\{m\}p\_\{j\}{\textbackslash}cdot x\_\{j\}\}    . Given a price-vector, the budget of an agent is the total price of his endowment,                         p         {$\cdot$}                    e                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p{\textbackslash}cdot e\_\{i\}\}    . A bundle is affordable for a buyer if the price of that bundle is at most the buyer's budget. I.e, a bundle                         x                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\}     is affordable for buyer                         i                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i\}     if                         p         {$\cdot$}         x         {$\leq$}         p         {$\cdot$}                    e                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p{\textbackslash}cdot x{\textbackslash}leq p{\textbackslash}cdot e\_\{i\}\}    . Each buyer has a preference relation over bundles, which can be represented by a utility function. The utility function of buyer                         i                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i\}     is denoted by                                    u                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle u\_\{i\}\}    .  The demand set of a buyer is the set of affordable bundles that maximize the buyer's utility among all affordable bundles, i.e.:                                                Demand                                   i                             (         p         )         :=         arg         ⁡                    max                        p             {$\cdot$}             x             {$\leq$}             p             {$\cdot$}                            e                                i                                                                    u                        i                             (         x         )                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle \{{\textbackslash}text\{Demand\}\}\_\{i\}(p):={\textbackslash}arg {\textbackslash}max \_\{p{\textbackslash}cdot x{\textbackslash}leq p{\textbackslash}cdot e\_\{i\}\}u\_\{i\}(x)\}    . A competitive equilibrium (CE) is a price-vector                                    p                        1                             ,         {\dots}         ,                    p                        m                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p\_\{1\},{\textbackslash}dots ,p\_\{m\}\}    in which it is possible to allocate, to each agent, a bundle from his demand-set, such that the total allocation exactly equals the supply of products. The corresponding prices are called market-clearing prices. A CE always exists, even in the more general Arrow--Debreu model. The main challenge is to find a CE.},
